WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.350
- Corpus Juris.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.030
Ad hominen tu quoque.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.130
Post hoc ergo propter hoc.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.420
Quod...

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.550
I know all of these things,
and it's so hard to say.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:09.870
Every once in a while

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.230
a genius changes the
legal landscape forever.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.570
Sometimes that genius isn't even a lawyer.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.160
And scholars will look back on history

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.010
as the days before "The Onion"
filed its Supreme Court brief

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.990
and the days after because
we are all changed.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.100
Now, it's not surprising

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.520
because according to "The
Onion's" Supreme Court brief,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.767
"'The Onion' rose from
its humble beginnings

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.137
"as a print newspaper in 1756

00:00:32.137 --> 00:00:35.317
"and now enjoys a daily
readership of 4.3 trillion

00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.387
"and has grown into the
single most powerful

00:00:37.387 --> 00:00:40.200
"and influential organization
in human history."

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.140
Yes, "The Onion" has filed
its first ever amicus brief,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.050
and it started with the
top, the U.S. Supreme Court.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.690
Its brief immediately
took law Twitter by storm.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.460
And I've read a lot of briefs in my time,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.100
and trust me, they're not
all as funny and precise

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:53.970
as this one.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.730
And people have asked if real
lawyers were actually involved

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.810
in this particular brief.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.587
Yes, the authors of this momentous brief,

00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:01.200
"The Onion's" head writer Mike Gillis

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.700
and "The Onion's" lawyers of record

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.150
Stephen J. van Stempvoort
and D. Andrew Portinga,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.550
who are real people, I
actually checked on that,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.800
pulled no punches in their brief writing.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.967
Quote, "'The Onion' knows
that the federal judiciary

00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:15.307
"is staffed entirely by total Latin dorks.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.617
"They quote Catullus in the
original Latin in chambers.

00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:19.837
"They sweetly whisper 'stare decisis'

00:01:19.837 --> 00:01:21.067
"to their spouse's ears.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.477
"They mutter 'cui bono' under their breath

00:01:22.477 --> 00:01:24.547
"while picking up after
their neighbors' dogs.

00:01:24.547 --> 00:01:26.557
"So 'The Onion' knew
that unless it pointed

00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:28.477
"to a suitably Latin rallying cry,

00:01:28.477 --> 00:01:29.827
"its brief would be operating

00:01:29.827 --> 00:01:32.220
"far outside the Court's vernacular."

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.110
I feel so seen.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.400
But the question must be asked,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.380
is this a great Supreme Court brief

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.860
or the greatest Supreme
Court brief in history?

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.360
But first we have to ask ourselves,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.000
why is "The Onion" filing
a friend of the court brief

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.590
with the Supreme Court?

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.630
Well, they're standing
up for Anthony Novak,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.240
an Ohio man who started mocking
his local police department

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.100
in March of 2016.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.320
Novak's Facebook page had the same name,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.640
cover photo, and profile photo

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.040
as the Parma Police
Department's official page,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.080
but it was designated a community page

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.270
and didn't have the official
blue check mark verifying it

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.560
as an official page.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.950
Novak's page displayed the
satirical slogan, "We no crime."

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.860
Now, Parma is Cleveland,
Ohio's biggest suburb

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.630
and it has long had a reputation

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.350
for being just a little bit
extra, and by that I mean

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.900
it has a well-documented
problem with racism.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.670
In the 1970s, the Justice
Department concluded

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.250
that Parma's near-manic
growth was a classic example

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.300
of white flight.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.040
The DOJ found that the
city of Parma engaged

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.690
in a pattern and practice
of racial discrimination

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.020
in housing in violation of
the Fair Housing Act of 1968,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.620
and the city didn't settle
with the DOJ until 1998.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.450
The Parma Police Department has been cited

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.000
for refusing to hire
minority police officers.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.130
Cleveland is 51% Black.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.220
Its largest suburb is
still less than 5% Black.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.020
And I'll note parenthetically
that one of the members

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.730
of Team LegalEagle is from Cleveland

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.130
and informs me that the
Parma police are notorious

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.950
for prosecuting drivers
for driving while Black.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.170
Now, legendary public
access host Ghoulardi,

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.330
father of director Paul Thomas Anderson,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.000
relentlessly mocked Parma for decades.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.830
Anthony Novak clearly knew this history

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.020
when he set out to mock the local cops.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.587
As "Cleveland Magazine" once said, quote,

00:03:05.587 --> 00:03:07.717
"Everyone in the country
makes fun of Cleveland,

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:09.930
"and everyone in Cleveland
makes fun of Parma."

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.090
Now, Novak's page was up for six hours,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.520
and during that time it
published six satirical posts.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.207
The first was an apology for, quote,

00:03:16.207 --> 00:03:18.697
"Failing to inform the
public about an armed robbery

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:21.060
"of a Subway sandwich
shop by a white man,"

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.777
while requesting information to, quote,

00:03:22.777 --> 00:03:24.877
"bring to justice an
African American woman

00:03:24.877 --> 00:03:27.780
"who was loitering in front of
the shop during the robbery."

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.520
Novak then published a fake announcement

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.950
that new police officers
would be recruited based on a,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.507
quote, "15 question multiple
choice definition test

00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:36.247
"followed by a hearing test,

00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:39.030
"and strongly encouraged
minorities to not apply."

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.060
He followed up with an update
about a pedophile reform event

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.220
promising that anyone who
made it through the stations

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.470
would be removed from
the sex offender registry

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.080
and accepted as an
honorary police officer.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.670
Now by now you are probably thinking

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.710
that surely everyone who saw these pages

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:53.790
knew it was a parody

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.400
and, of course, was protected
by the First Amendment.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.040
But that's not how the
Parma police saw it.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.260
They got a few phone calls
from confused citizens.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.730
They immediately posted a warning

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.040
on the police department's
real page letting people know

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.540
that the other page was fake

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.700
and that the department was investigating.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.410
The comments to the notice reveal

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.050
that most citizens understood
what was happening.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.430
Now, Novak thought it would be funny

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.890
to post the police notice
on his page verbatim,

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.030
so that's what he did.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.070
Officer Kevin Riley didn't like that,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.480
and so he went on the local news

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.520
where he said that the cops
were launching an investigation

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.260
into the page's owner.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.670
Riley described the page as, quote-

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.710
- But frankly, they're
crude, they're demeaning,

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.180
and they're very inflammatory.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.160
Our concern right now is public safety.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.770
- Now as legally idiotic
as those statements were,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.680
Riley's comments did prompt
Novak to take his page down,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.660
but the Parma officials did not let up.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.260
Instead, the police subpoenaed
Facebook to unmask Novak.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.250
The page wasn't even up

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.320
by the time that they
discovered Novak's identity.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.430
Now, once they knew it was Novak,

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.590
they consulted with the
prosecutor Timothy Dobeck.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.330
Dobeck and the officers
searched for a crime

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.310
to fit the situation and ultimately landed

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.790
on Ohio Revised Code, Section 2909.04 B,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.137
a felony statute prohibiting
the knowing, quote,

00:04:59.137 --> 00:05:01.627
"Use of any computer
to disrupt, interrupt,

00:05:01.627 --> 00:05:04.620
"or impair the functions
of any police operation."

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.680
Now, how did Novak disrupt
the police department?

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.570
His parody caused 11 people

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.490
to call up the station in confusion.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.410
But that was enough for a local magistrate

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.030
who found that the cops had probable cause

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.620
to search Novak's house.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.620
The police then conducted a
SWAT raid on Novak's home,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.410
confiscating his gaming
consoles, computers, and tablets.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.650
Police arrested Novak and threw
him in jail for four days.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.840
Detective Thomas Connor
told the grand jury

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.540
that the callers, quote, "honest
to God" actually believed

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:31.770
the page was real.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.300
The grand jury indicted Novak

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.980
for disrupting public services,

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.230
a fourth degree felony that
carried a maximum penalty

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:38.460
of 18 months in prison.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.620
But not surprisingly,
later during a deposition

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.210
Connor admitted that none of the callers

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.340
thought the parody Facebook page was real,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.510
but the damage was done.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.070
The city continues to insist to this day

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.360
that 10 of those calls wasted
precious dispatch time.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.263
But let's be real,

00:05:53.257 --> 00:05:56.227
"Parma dispatchers probably
get more useless phone calls

00:05:56.227 --> 00:06:00.060
"when Rudy's Strudel runs
out of donuts on paczki day."

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.190
I guess that's a Cleveland joke.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.870
Now, Novak stood trial

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.810
and, lucky for him,
the jury acquitted him.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.870
And after he was acquitted, he
then sued the city of Parma,

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.550
the police department,
and the police officers

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.050
for violating his civil rights,

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:15.420
because that's exactly what they did.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:16.860
The police officers, not surprisingly,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.440
raised qualified immunity as a defense.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.540
Now, I've talked about
qualified immunity in the past,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.390
but here's a recap of this
judicially invented doctrine.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.130
Qualified immunity
protects state officials

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.230
from being held accountable
on a federal level

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.870
for violating a person's
constitutional rights.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.600
Under this doctrine, an officer
or state actor is immune

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.360
from civil liability if a
court has not previously stated

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.400
that the officer's act
was unconstitutional

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.290
in a prior court decision.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.840
It's an idea that if there
isn't a court decision

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.940
on this type of constitutional violation,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.470
then they don't have notice

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.200
that they were violating
someone's constitutional rights.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.200
Now, qualified immunity only
allows lawsuits to move forward

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.947
if state officials violated a, quote,

00:06:53.947 --> 00:06:56.910
"clearly established statutory
or constitutional right."

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:59.340
And when determining
whether or not a right

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.330
was clearly established,

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.060
courts consider whether a
hypothetical reasonable officer

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.860
would've known that
the defendant's conduct

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.720
violated the plaintiff's rights.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.490
What started as a way to ensure

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:10.140
that officials were not held liable

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.520
for things that they had no way of knowing

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.740
were unconstitutional has now become

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.450
really a serious impediment

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.760
for holding police officers accountable.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.680
The Federal District
Court for Northern Ohio

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.620
denied the Parma officers'
motion for qualified immunity,

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.410
but the Sixth Circuit eventually
overturned that decision.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.090
The court held it was
not clearly established

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.760
that Novak's copying of
the department's notice

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.250
onto his page and the
deletion of user comments

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.720
were protected speech.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.127
Under the disruption statute, quote,

00:07:36.127 --> 00:07:37.747
"The officers could reasonably believe

00:07:37.747 --> 00:07:40.657
"that some of Novak's Facebook
activity was not parody,

00:07:40.657 --> 00:07:44.040
"not protected, and fair
grounds for probable cause."

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.690
The Sixth Circuit put special value

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.300
on the fact that a prosecutor
and magistrate agreed

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:49.500
that there was probable cause

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.570
that Novak violated the statute.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.700
Now with that somewhat
depressing background in mind,

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.350
we can turn to how in the world

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.240
does this involve "The Onion"?

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.160
Well, when someone files an amicus brief

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.150
they have to start by stating

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:01.590
why they have an interest in the case

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.150
that the court should consider.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.530
So the brief starts out by discussing

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.290
the interests of the amicus,
the friend of the court,

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.280
in this case "The Onion."

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.867
Quote, "'The Onion' is the
world's leading news publication

00:08:10.867 --> 00:08:13.417
"offering highly acclaimed,
universally revered coverage

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:15.187
"of breaking national, international,

00:08:15.187 --> 00:08:16.650
"and local news events."

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.937
Quote, "'The Onion's'
keen fact-driven reportage

00:08:18.937 --> 00:08:21.727
"has been cited favorably
by one or more local courts,

00:08:21.727 --> 00:08:25.170
"as well as Iran and the
Chinese state-run media."

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.003
Now, I love this part

00:08:26.003 --> 00:08:28.350
because it goes to the heart
of what makes good parody,

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.570
the ability of the satirist
to actually make people think

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:31.650
it might be real.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.540
And "The Onion" has tricked many people

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.580
into reporting its stories as fact.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.120
And back in 2012, Iran's Fars
News Agency published a story

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.880
claiming that a Gallup poll
found that rural white Americans

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.540
preferred Iranian President
Mahmoud Ahmadinejad

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:47.520
over President Barack Obama.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.040
Although that feels like
it might have been true,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.500
unfortunately Ahmadinejad was ineligible

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.840
for U.S. presidency, so we'll
never know if his stance

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.390
about women covering their
hair would've gained traction

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:01.050
in rural Indiana, though
I worry that it would.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.690
Chinese state media also
thought that "The Onion's" story

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.747
declaring Kim Jong-Un the
sexiest man alive was real.

00:09:07.747 --> 00:09:09.907
"The Onion" wrote that Kim was, quote,

00:09:09.907 --> 00:09:13.207
"Blessed with an air of
power that masks (chuckles)

00:09:13.207 --> 00:09:16.117
"an unmistakably cute, cuddly side.

00:09:16.117 --> 00:09:18.847
"Kim made this newspaper's
editorial board swoon

00:09:18.847 --> 00:09:22.027
"with his impeccable fashion
sense, short hairstyle,

00:09:22.027 --> 00:09:23.880
"and, of course, that famous smile."

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.080
Well, look, when you put it that way,

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.440
it's easy to see why the 45th
president had such a crush.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.270
Now, "The Onion" brief also points out

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.940
that it has a good track record
of predicting future events.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.647
Quote, "One such coup was
'The Onion's' scoop revealing

00:09:35.647 --> 00:09:37.687
"that a former president
kept nuclear secrets

00:09:37.687 --> 00:09:39.457
"strewn around his beach home's basement

00:09:39.457 --> 00:09:41.790
"three years before it even happened."

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.530
Fact check, true. (chuckles)

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.230
Now, "The Onion" really
did write a story in 2017

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.237
about the nuclear briefcase
being left behind at Mar-a-Lago.

00:09:49.237 --> 00:09:52.087
"Noting that it had already
been there for almost two weeks,

00:09:52.087 --> 00:09:54.247
"Mar-a-Lago assistant
manager Chris Mahoney

00:09:54.247 --> 00:09:56.017
"reportedly wondered if anyone was coming

00:09:56.017 --> 00:09:57.397
"to collect the nuclear briefcase

00:09:57.397 --> 00:09:59.130
"from the club's lost and found."

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.930
Anyway, "The Onion" filed this brief

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.730
to protect the rights of parody creators

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.220
who, as a class, would
prefer not to be arrested

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.210
for being funny.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.687
Quote, "'The Onion' files this brief

00:10:07.687 --> 00:10:10.087
"to protect its continued
ability to create fiction

00:10:10.087 --> 00:10:12.277
"that may ultimately merge into reality.

00:10:12.277 --> 00:10:14.197
"As the globe's premiere parodists,

00:10:14.197 --> 00:10:16.867
"'The Onion's' writers also
have a self-serving interest

00:10:16.867 --> 00:10:18.307
"in preventing political authorities

00:10:18.307 --> 00:10:20.107
"from imprisoning humorists.

00:10:20.107 --> 00:10:22.027
"This brief is submitted in the interest

00:10:22.027 --> 00:10:24.240
"of at least mitigating
their future punishment."

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.280
And the brief zeros in on
the Sixth Circuit's holding

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.900
that a parodist would not be protected

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.670
unless they let the
audience in on the joke

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.377
by warning them that
their parody is not true.

00:10:32.377 --> 00:10:33.457
"The Onion" notes that, quote,

00:10:33.457 --> 00:10:34.987
"Some forms of comedy don't work

00:10:34.987 --> 00:10:36.817
"unless the comedian is
able to tell the joke

00:10:36.817 --> 00:10:37.987
"with a straight face.

00:10:37.987 --> 00:10:40.177
"Parody is the quintessential example.

00:10:40.177 --> 00:10:43.207
"Parodists intentionally
inhabit the rhetorical form

00:10:43.207 --> 00:10:46.237
"of their target in order
to exaggerate or implode it,

00:10:46.237 --> 00:10:47.707
"and by doing so demonstrate

00:10:47.707 --> 00:10:50.040
"the target's illogic or absurdity."

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.870
Now, the hilarity of
the brief is an example

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:54.630
of the thing it seeks to
protect: an ultras serious take

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:58.170
on a real thing that by its
nature will fool some people.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:00.217
Or as "The Onion's" lawyers put it, quote,

00:11:00.217 --> 00:11:01.117
"For parody to work,

00:11:01.117 --> 00:11:03.660
"it has to plausibly mimic the original."

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.977
And as "The Onion" points
out in its brief, quote,

00:11:05.977 --> 00:11:08.257
"Tu stultus es. You are dumb.

00:11:08.257 --> 00:11:10.717
"These Latin words have
been 'The Onion's' motto

00:11:10.717 --> 00:11:12.847
"and guiding light since
it was founded in 1988

00:11:12.847 --> 00:11:14.557
"as America's finest news source,

00:11:14.557 --> 00:11:17.197
"leading its writers toward
the paper's singular purpose

00:11:17.197 --> 00:11:18.787
"of pointing out that its readers

00:11:18.787 --> 00:11:20.730
"are deeply gullible people."

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.500
And they're not just crapping
on their own audience.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:23.977
They're making an important
point that, quote,

00:11:23.977 --> 00:11:25.297
"Tricking readers into believing

00:11:25.297 --> 00:11:26.647
"that they're seeing a serious rendering

00:11:26.647 --> 00:11:27.907
"of some specific form,

00:11:27.907 --> 00:11:30.337
"a pop song lyric, a newspaper
article, a police beat,

00:11:30.337 --> 00:11:32.527
"and then allowing them to
laugh at their own gullibility

00:11:32.527 --> 00:11:34.117
"when they realize that
they've fallen victim

00:11:34.117 --> 00:11:36.630
"to one of the oldest tricks
in the history of rhetoric."

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:39.690
And despite the literal
falsity of parody and satire,

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.880
it literally has to be false to work,

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.150
it enjoys First Amendment
protection, as it should.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.940
And when assessing whether
satirical speech is defamation,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.130
the courts consider
what a reasonable reader

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:52.080
would've understood the
author to have said,

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.450
not the literal meaning of the words.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.870
And the capacity to fool
the audience is essential.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.340
So in other words, a person...

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:00.360
Billy.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.280
No, you can't be up here.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.020
So in other words, a person
doesn't have to publish

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.850
a disclaimer that the writing is fake.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.220
That would ruin the joke.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.170
Imagine how it would've
ruined the punchline

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.760
of Jonathan Swift's "A Modest Proposal"

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.530
which argued with a straight face

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:16.920
that the starving Irish should
just eat their own babies.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.200
And if you're worried about
"The Onion" being humble

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.780
in comparing itself to Jonathan Swift,

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.010
well, you don't need to be.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:23.920
They write, quote, "To be clear,

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.867
"'The Onion' is not
trying to compare itself

00:12:25.867 --> 00:12:26.857
"to Jonathan Swift.

00:12:26.857 --> 00:12:28.687
"Its writers are far more talented

00:12:28.687 --> 00:12:30.517
"and their output will be read long after

00:12:30.517 --> 00:12:33.607
"that hack Swift's has been
lost to the sands of time.

00:12:33.607 --> 00:12:35.827
"Still, 'The Onion' and its
writers share with Swift

00:12:35.827 --> 00:12:37.777
"the common goal of replicating a form

00:12:37.777 --> 00:12:40.590
"precisely in order to
critique it from within."

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.210
And as "The Onion" points out,

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.360
Swift's goals was the same,
to replicate the form,

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.980
the boring economic paper,

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.170
in order to critique it from within.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.210
And that's why the full
title of Swift's paper

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:54.127
was "A Modest Proposal for
Preventing the Children

00:12:54.127 --> 00:12:56.887
"of Poor People from Being
a Burden to Their Parents

00:12:56.887 --> 00:12:59.880
"or Country and for Making
Them Beneficial to the Public."

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.650
And "The Onion" writers do this every day

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.420
to hilarious effect.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.797
The brief points to an
"Onion" article titled, quote,

00:13:05.797 --> 00:13:08.790
"The Supreme Court Rules
Supreme Court Rules,"

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.730
which is meant to mimic the
form of "The Associated Press."

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.377
It starts with the sentence,
"In a landmark decision Monday,

00:13:14.377 --> 00:13:17.760
"the United States Supreme
Court ruled 8-1 that it rules."

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.800
And "The Onion" follows
"The Associated Press" form

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.090
straight through into the article.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:22.927
It says, quote, "Justice Anthony Kennedy,

00:13:22.927 --> 00:13:25.373
"writing on behalf of the
majority, noted that, quote,

00:13:25.373 --> 00:13:28.147
"'while the U.S. Constitution
guarantees equality of power

00:13:28.147 --> 00:13:30.607
"among the executive, legislative,
and judicial branches,

00:13:30.607 --> 00:13:32.377
"it most definitely does not guarantee

00:13:32.377 --> 00:13:33.787
"equality of coolness,

00:13:33.787 --> 00:13:36.247
"and in this regard, the
judicial branch kicks

00:13:36.247 --> 00:13:39.810
"that which can be construed
as total and complete ass.'"

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:43.140
So the power of the parody
rises from that dissonance

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.780
into which the reader has been drawn.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.250
And "The Onion's" brief argues

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.130
that because parody mimics the real thing

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.420
it has even more power to
critique the real thing.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:52.837
Quote, "Parodists can take apart

00:13:52.837 --> 00:13:54.487
"an authoritarian's cult of personality,

00:13:54.487 --> 00:13:55.687
"point out the rhetorical tricks

00:13:55.687 --> 00:13:57.757
"that politicians use to
mislead their constituents,

00:13:57.757 --> 00:14:00.427
"and even undercut a
government institution's

00:14:00.427 --> 00:14:02.700
"real world attempts at propaganda."

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:05.790
For "The Onion," this has
worked time and time again

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:07.650
since its articles tend to expose leaders

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.560
who have no ability to
distinguish fact from fiction.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.510
The brief singles out one of them,

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.647
Republican Congressman
John Fleming, who, quote,

00:14:14.647 --> 00:14:16.807
"Believed that he needed
to warn his constituents

00:14:16.807 --> 00:14:19.537
"of a dangerous escalation
of the pro-choice movement

00:14:19.537 --> 00:14:21.607
"after reading 'The Onion's' headline,

00:14:21.607 --> 00:14:25.320
"'Planned Parenthood Opens
$8 Billion Abortionplex.'"

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.270
Of course, Yelp reviewers
got in on the gag

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:29.610
and reviewed the fake Kansas Abortionplex,

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.920
which convinced other readers
that this was a real thing

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.920
and, quote, "We taxpayers,
we're paying for it."

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.630
The legendary Abortionplex story

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:39.210
is still fooling people in 2022.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:40.770
And although the brief tongue-in-cheek

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.390
called "Onion" readers stupid,

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:43.410
it backtracks on that claim

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.750
in explaining the mental
capacity of reasonable readers

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.097
who, quote, "Do not need
to be told explicitly

00:14:48.097 --> 00:14:49.717
"what they have no serious trouble

00:14:49.717 --> 00:14:51.390
"figuring out for themselves."

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.010
And the brief notes that the Supreme Court

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.930
has already indicated that
disclaimers aren't needed.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:56.460
They refer to a footnote

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.770
of the opinion of Campbell
versus Acuff-Rose,

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:01.470
which, of course, was a
very famous fair use case

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.570
where the dispute was
about whether the rap group

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.580
2 Live Crew had a right
to publish a parody

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:07.470
of Roy Orbison's "Pretty Woman."

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.720
And in that case, the
court said that 2 Live Crew

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.630
need not label their whole
album or even the song a parody

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:14.730
in order to claim fair use protection,

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.010
nor should 2 Live Crew be penalized

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.851
for this being its first parodic essay.

00:15:19.851 --> 00:15:21.601
♪ Big hairy woman ♪

00:15:21.601 --> 00:15:22.953
♪ You need to shave that stuff ♪

00:15:22.953 --> 00:15:25.950
- Parody serves its goals,
whether labeled or not,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:28.950
and there's no reason to require
parody to state the obvious

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.260
or even the reasonably perceived.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.640
And "The Onion" argues convincingly

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:33.810
that the Sixth Circuit's holding

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.730
is contrary to the case law.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.687
The court ruled that
the Parma cops, quote,

00:15:37.687 --> 00:15:38.677
"Could reasonably believe

00:15:38.677 --> 00:15:41.917
"that some of Novak's Facebook
activity was not parody,

00:15:41.917 --> 00:15:44.227
"primarily because Mr.
Novak deleted comments

00:15:44.227 --> 00:15:46.140
"that made clear the page was fake."

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.780
But the brief argues that the
lack of explicit disclaimer

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:51.150
makes no difference to
whether a reasonable reader

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:53.580
would discern that this speech was parody.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.380
And "The Onion" takes issue
with the Sixth Circuit's view

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.060
that the search warrants
insulates the police

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.460
from liability since other
authority figures also share

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.830
the same wrongheaded view of free speech.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.297
Quote, "This was not a
close call on the facts.

00:16:04.297 --> 00:16:06.517
"Mr. Novak's spoof
Facebook posts advertised

00:16:06.517 --> 00:16:08.257
"that the Parma Police
Department was hosting

00:16:08.257 --> 00:16:09.997
"a pedophile reform event

00:16:09.997 --> 00:16:12.367
"in which successful
participants could be removed

00:16:12.367 --> 00:16:13.987
"from the sex offender registry

00:16:13.987 --> 00:16:15.997
"and become honorary
members of the department

00:16:15.997 --> 00:16:18.037
"after completing puzzles and quizzes;

00:16:18.037 --> 00:16:19.537
"that the department had discovered

00:16:19.537 --> 00:16:21.247
"an experimental technique for abortions

00:16:21.247 --> 00:16:23.617
"and would be providing
them to teens for free

00:16:23.617 --> 00:16:24.667
"in a police van;

00:16:24.667 --> 00:16:26.827
"that the department was
soliciting job applicants,

00:16:26.827 --> 00:16:28.027
"but that minorities were, quote,

00:16:28.027 --> 00:16:29.947
"'strongly encouraged' not to apply;

00:16:29.947 --> 00:16:32.347
"and that the department
was banning city residents

00:16:32.347 --> 00:16:34.897
"from feeding homeless
people in, quote, 'an attempt

00:16:34.897 --> 00:16:37.207
"to have the homeless population
eventually leave our city

00:16:37.207 --> 00:16:38.370
"due to starvation.'"

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:40.290
So yeah, you'd have to
be pretty dumb to think

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:43.230
that the Facebook posts
were real and also criminal.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:45.540
Novak can't help it if a magistrate judge

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:46.680
and city attorney are closer

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.090
to the lowest common denominator

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:50.400
than the average reasonable reader.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:51.810
And "The Onion" also argues

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.990
that parodists should not be
prosecuted for telling jokes.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.730
Now this seems self-explanatory,

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.340
so "The Onion" lawyers
crafted a couple paragraphs

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:00.870
just to keep your attention.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.757
Quote, "This is the 15th page
of a convoluted legal filing

00:17:03.757 --> 00:17:04.957
"intended to deconstruct

00:17:04.957 --> 00:17:06.787
"the societal implications of parody,

00:17:06.787 --> 00:17:09.307
"so the reader's attention is
almost certainly wandering.

00:17:09.307 --> 00:17:10.327
"That's understandable.

00:17:10.327 --> 00:17:12.727
"So here's a paragraph of
gripping legal analysis

00:17:12.727 --> 00:17:15.307
"to ensure that every
jurist who reads this brief

00:17:15.307 --> 00:17:17.917
"is appropriately impressed
by the logic of its argument

00:17:17.917 --> 00:17:19.987
"and the lucidity of its pros.

00:17:19.987 --> 00:17:21.277
"Bona vacantia.

00:17:21.277 --> 00:17:23.077
"De bonis asportatis.

00:17:23.077 --> 00:17:24.457
"Writ of certiorari.

00:17:24.457 --> 00:17:25.357
"De minimus.

00:17:25.357 --> 00:17:26.857
"Jus accrescendi.

00:17:26.857 --> 00:17:28.447
"Forum non conveniens.

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:29.707
"Corpus Juris.

00:17:29.707 --> 00:17:31.357
"Ad hominem tu quoque.

00:17:31.357 --> 00:17:33.487
"Post hoc ergo propter hoc.

00:17:33.487 --> 00:17:34.950
"Quod..."

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:36.847
I know all of these things,
and it's so hard to say.

00:17:36.847 --> 00:17:39.262
"Quod est demonstrandum.

00:17:39.262 --> 00:17:40.095
"Actus reus.

00:17:40.095 --> 00:17:41.955
"Scandalum magnatum.

00:17:41.955 --> 00:17:44.137
"Pactum reservati dominii.

00:17:44.137 --> 00:17:44.970
"See what happened?

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:46.507
"This brief itself went from a discussion

00:17:46.507 --> 00:17:48.667
"of parody's function and
the quite serious historical

00:17:48.667 --> 00:17:50.707
"and legal arguments in
favor of strong protections

00:17:50.707 --> 00:17:54.067
"for parodic speech to a curve
ball mocking the way legalese

00:17:54.067 --> 00:17:56.197
"can be both impenetrably boring

00:17:56.197 --> 00:17:58.927
"and belie the hollowness
of a legal position.

00:17:58.927 --> 00:18:00.937
"That's the setup and
punchline idea again.

00:18:00.937 --> 00:18:02.467
"It would not have worked quite as well

00:18:02.467 --> 00:18:04.417
"if this brief had said the following:

00:18:04.417 --> 00:18:06.877
"'Hello there, reader, we're
about to write an amicus brief

00:18:06.877 --> 00:18:08.227
"about the value of parody.

00:18:08.227 --> 00:18:09.877
"Buckle up, because
we're going to be doing

00:18:09.877 --> 00:18:11.557
"some fairly outre things,

00:18:11.557 --> 00:18:14.370
"including commenting on
this text's form itself.'"

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:16.230
So yeah, it's not good
to punish funny people

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.100
for saying funny things,

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.230
even if those things may be offensive.

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:20.347
And "The Onion" promises to, quote,

00:18:20.347 --> 00:18:22.057
"Continue its socially valuable role

00:18:22.057 --> 00:18:23.587
"bringing the disinfectant of sunlight

00:18:23.587 --> 00:18:24.727
"into the halls of power.

00:18:24.727 --> 00:18:25.867
"And it would vastly prefer

00:18:25.867 --> 00:18:28.147
"that sunlight not be
measured out to its writers

00:18:28.147 --> 00:18:30.570
"in 15 minute increments
in an exercise yard."

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.490
And this YouTube channel agrees.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.980
Now normally a lawyer's
conclusion is a pretty dry summary

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:36.480
of what they want the court to do,

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.360
but here "The Onion" aims high once again.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.887
Quote, "The petition for
certiorari should be granted,

00:18:41.887 --> 00:18:43.357
"the rights of the people vindicated,

00:18:43.357 --> 00:18:45.337
"and various historical wrongs remedied.

00:18:45.337 --> 00:18:47.377
"'The Onion' would
welcome any of the three,

00:18:47.377 --> 00:18:49.140
"particularly the first."

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:50.010
So what happens next?

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:51.330
Well, the Supreme Court will decide

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.100
whether to take Novak's case or not

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:57.150
and potentially overturn the
Sixth Circuit's decision.

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:57.983
If it does not,

00:18:57.983 --> 00:19:00.690
then the Sixth Circuit
decision remains good law

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.430
despite it being bad law.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:03.263
But you'll be able to read

00:19:03.263 --> 00:19:05.160
all about the Supreme
Court's next decision

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:06.930
in today's sponsor, Morning Brew,

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:08.520
because that's my favorite way to catch up

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.260
with pop culture and the news.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.670
In fact, you could have already learned

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.950
about "The Onion's" brief
because they covered it

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.990
and I read it in my email.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.540
I've actually been subscribed
to Morning Brew for forever.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:20.700
It's one of my absolute
favorite newsletters,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.500
and I'm thrilled to tell you why.

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:24.060
Because Morning Brew, if
you didn't know already,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.790
is a free daily newsletter
delivered Monday through Sunday.

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:30.000
It gets you up to speed on
business, finance, tech, and law

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.550
